<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="65596" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/EALC/495/65596.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2025" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025.xml">2025</calendarYear><term id="120251" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring.xml">Spring 2025</term><subject id="EALC" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/EALC.xml">East Asian Language and Culture</subject><course id="495" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/EALC/495.xml">Topics in Asian Religions</course></parents><sectionNumber>CC1</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Lotus Sutra &amp; Japanese Culture</sectionTitle><creditHours>4 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Perhaps no other Buddhist scripture has had more impact on East Asian Culture than The Lotus Sutra. After reading the sutra and recent scholarship concerning its production and reception in India and China, we will examine the fundamental role the sutra played in Japanese Buddhism, with special attention to medieval Tendai and Nichiren. Attention will also be given to the impact the Lotus Sutra has had on art, literature, gender and Japanese society.</sectionText><sectionNotes>Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign.</sectionNotes><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>CrossListOpen (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2025-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2025-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>01:00 PM</start><end>03:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>F      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>1118</roomNumber><buildingName>Literatures, Cultures, &amp; Ling</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Callahan" firstName="C">Callahan, C</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>